/-!
# Signal Double Ratchet: Extraction to Security

Instantiates the hax-extracted `DoubleratchetDeps` of the crate `doubleratchet-hax`
(<https://github.com/spitters/signal-hax>) with libcrux-specs primitives and carries the game of
the extracted session to the Signal double ratchet of `Signal.DoubleRatchet_UC`.

```
doubleratchet-hax                        (Rust: the typestate session, generic in the
        |  haxpipeT --emit-certified       trait DoubleRatchetDeps)
Doubleratchet_haxpipe                    (DoubleratchetDeps: hmac_sha256, hkdf_extract,
        |                                  hkdf_expand, x25519, aead_encrypt, aead_decrypt)
        |  defaultDoubleratchetDeps        (libcrux-specs HMAC-SHA256, HKDF-SHA256, X25519)
        |  defaultDrHom                    (vecToArrayInt carries them to the model)
ExtractedCorrespondence                  (sm_extracted over Session_* = encoded sm_real)
        |  signalDR_extracted_advantage_le
Signal.DoubleRatchet_UC                  (DH CKA, HKDF root step, FS-AEAD)
```

The extraction computes on byte arrays and the model on `Vector UInt8 32`. The instance
`defaultDoubleratchetDeps` decodes its arguments (`decodeKey`, the inverse of
`vecToArrayInt`), applies the libcrux-specs operation and encodes the result: `hmac_sha256` is
`hmac_sha256_32`, `hkdf_extract` and `hkdf_expand` are `hkdf_extract_32` and `hkdf_expand_32`,
and `x25519` is `x25519_scalarmult`. Encryption is XOR: `aead_encrypt` and `aead_decrypt` are
`xorBytes`, the XOR of the decoded 32-byte key and the first 32 entries of the text (`wxorEnc`
on words). The trait's `aead_encrypt` takes texts of up to `MAX_PLAINTEXT_LEN = 256` bytes;
`xorBytes` reads 32 of them, and the theorems here use it at the 32-byte encodings
`vecToArrayInt` of words only.

The Diffie–Hellman CKA of the model is over the prime-order subgroup `G25519 B` of Curve25519
generated by a point `B` of order `ℓ`, with exponents `ZMod ℓ` and elements encoded by their
u-coordinate `encU`. The hypothesis is `X25519Hom B`, the RFC 7748 correctness of the libcrux
X25519 on that subgroup (a `Fact` for the instances). The extraction computes X25519 with a
32-byte key `sk`, which RFC 7748 clamps; the clamped scalars `clampE sk` reach at most `2^251`
residues modulo `ℓ`, so the model draws the secret key `(sk, b)` of `Sk = Vector UInt8 32 × Bool`
with the exponent `signedE (sk, b) = ± clampE sk`. The extraction sees the bytes of `sk` only,
and `encU` identifies `T` and `-T`, so the sign changes no encoded value; the law `signedClamp`
of the signed clamped scalar is within `Δ25519 ≤ 2^-126` of uniform on `ZMod ℓ`
(`signedClampCert`), and each of the `msgs.length + 1` exponent draws of the CKA is charged
`Δ25519`.

`signalDR_e2e_uc_secure`: the game of the extracted session at `defaultDoubleratchetDeps`
UC-emulates the encoded ideal game, through the class route (`PureGame`, `GameAdvBoundC`,
`UCFromGameC.uc`); the bound for adversary `A` and environment `Z` is `signalDRBound` at
`envReduction A Z` precomposed with the encoding of the views (`encodedRed`): the DDH advantages
in `G25519 B`, the PRG advantages of the HKDF root step and the FS-AEAD advantages of the
libcrux symmetric ratchet against explicit reductions, summed over the epochs, plus
`(msgs.length + 1) * Δ25519`.

`signalDR_extracted_corrupt_advantage_le` and `signalDR_extracted_adaptive_advantage_le`: the
session functions of the crate at `defaultDoubleratchetDeps`, as the secure-messaging scheme
`signalDRLibcruxSM B` of `ExtractedScheme` (`Uninitialized_establish` opens the initiator's
first epoch, the halves of `Session_dh_ratchet` open the other epochs and receive them), in the
ACD corruption game with forward secrecy and post-compromise security and in the game with an
adaptive challenge epoch. Under `X25519Hom B` the scheme simulates the ACD construction over the
Diffie–Hellman CKA `dhCKAOf (G25519 B) signedE`, whose secret keys are the libcrux keys
`(sk, b)`; the bound is that of `sm_corrupt_advantage_le` (`sm_adaptive_corrupt_advantage_le`)
at explicit reductions of the encoded adversary. A corruption reveals the bytes of the secret
key, which the exponent `signedE (sk, b)` does not determine. In the corruption game with
`2 ≤ ΔFS`, `2 ≤ ΔPCS` the CKA terms are DDH advantages in `G25519 B` of the reductions
`ckaCorruptDDHRedOf signedE ΔFS ΔPCS`, each plus `4 Δ25519` for the two exponent draws of the
epochs `t* - 1` and `t*` in the real and in the ideal game (`dhCKAOf_corrupt_advantage_le` at
`signedClampCert`). The bound of the adaptive game (with `2 ≤ ΔFS`, `1 ≤ ΔPCS`) keeps the CKA
corruption advantages of `dhCKAOf (G25519 B) signedE` at the guessing reductions of the epochs.

## References

* J. Alwen, S. Coretti, Y. Dodis, "The Double Ratchet: Security Notions, Proofs, and
  Modularization for the Signal Protocol", Eurocrypt 2019: the secure-messaging game and its
  decomposition into CKA, PRF-PRNG and FS-AEAD.
* R. Canetti, P. Jain, M. Swanberg, M. Varia, "Universally Composable End-to-End Secure
  Messaging", CRYPTO 2022 (ePrint 2022/376). The UC statement here is the lift of the
  secure-messaging game through `UCFromGameC`, not the functionality of that paper.
* T. Perrin, M. Marlinspike, "The Double Ratchet Algorithm", Signal specification, 2016.
-/

